WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.025
Service teams and divisions
aren't alternatives.

2
00:00:03.025 --> 00:00:04.779
Most agencies combine them,

3
00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:09.050
because a grant can be scoped by
service and by address space at once.

4
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.600
Again, it's illustrative, using the
worked example's four divisions.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.441
The central DNS team keeps agency
dot gov, internal and external,

6
00:00:18.441 --> 00:00:23.100
and DNSSEC signing, which only
the Grid Master or its HSM can do.

7
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.028
The central IPAM team keeps
the top-level blocks as network

8
00:00:27.028 --> 00:00:30.725
containers and allocates each
division its slash twelve.

9
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:36.489
Divisions take over DHCP: each division's
admins get Member DHCP and restart on

10
00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:42.075
their own DHCP members, and read-write
on their ranges, inside their own space.

11
00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.537
Read the grid by row.

12
00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:47.438
HQ's DHCP admins work in ten slash twelve.

13
00:00:47.438 --> 00:00:53.613
LEB serves its own zone from its own
members, with Member DNS and DHCP on them.

14
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:56.463
REG's Windows servers are
managed from the Grid,

15
00:00:56.463 --> 00:00:59.387
so its admins get rights
on those server objects.

16
00:00:59.387 --> 00:01:03.500
And LAB, with overlapping space,
has its own network view,

17
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.775
its own DHCP members, and a delegated
zone whose DS records go to central.

18
00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:11.625
On Universal DDI the picture changes.

19
00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:16.013
An access policy binds a role,
a user group and an access view.

20
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:22.161
Whether a division can have separate DNS
and DHCP roles depends on a documentation

21
00:01:22.161 --> 00:01:26.795
conflict: one Infoblox page lists
DDI IPAM, DNS and DHCP roles,

22
00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:32.676
while another says only the four DDI IPAM
roles are supported in this release.

23
00:01:32.676 --> 00:01:34.475
Confirm with Infoblox.

24
00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:38.760
DHCP objects can't be assigned to
access views directly; ranges,

25
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.787
subnets and fixed addresses inherit
their access view from their parent.

26
00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:47.475
And divisions can't administer
their own users or roles.

27
00:01:47.475 --> 00:01:50.425
One reporting caution
applies however you split.

28
00:01:50.425 --> 00:01:54.804
On NIOS, report and search data
ignore object permissions,

29
00:01:54.804 --> 00:02:00.825
so any team or division with the Reporting
app sees agency-wide DNS and DHCP data.

30
00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:06.767
Sources: Illustrative design synthesized
from Infoblox NIOS: Object Permissions,

31
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:12.193
DNS and DHCP Permissions on Grid Members,
Network Views, Network Containers,

32
00:02:12.193 --> 00:02:17.533
Delegated Zones, Configuring DNSSEC on
a Grid, Managing Microsoft Servers,

33
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:22.614
Reporting permissions; Infoblox
Portal: About Access Views, Supported

34
00:02:22.614 --> 00:02:28.270
Objects for Access Views, Setting Up
Access Control, Creating Access Policies.
